### A · The discipline of Gender Studies

1. **Gender Studies** studies how society **makes** femininity and masculinity and how this shares out **power** — it is not "the study of women only".
2. **Sex** refers to **anatomical and physiological** differences; **gender** refers to **social and cultural** roles (MCQ 2025).
3. "Social and cultural expectations of being male or female" describe a **gender role**, not sex (MCQ 2025).
4. The belief that "boys should play with trucks" is an example of the **social construction of gender** (MCQ 2025).
5. The four key ideas in any definition of Gender Studies are: gender is **made**, it is about **power**, the field is **multidisciplinary**, and gender **intersects** with other identities.
6. **Androcentrism** means treating **men as the normal human** and women as the exception.
7. **Patriarchy** is a **hierarchical system dominated by males** (MCQ 2023) — literally "rule of the father".
8. **Misogyny** means **hatred of or contempt for women** (MCQ 2018).
9. **Praxis** means joining **study with action** — a key feature of Gender Studies.
10. **Carol Gilligan** (*In a Different Voice*, ==1982==) found an **"ethic of care"** in girls that old theories had ranked as weaker.
11. **Marilyn Waring** (*If Women Counted*, ==1988==) showed that **GDP ignores unpaid work** such as cooking, cleaning and care.
12. "**Add women and stir**" describes the early, weak method of simply adding women to old subjects without changing their frameworks.
13. **Joan Wallach Scott** (==1986==) called gender "a **primary way of signifying relationships of power**".
14. **Feminist research** aims at **addressing women's issues from women's perspectives** (MCQ 2023).
15. The term **"interactive methodology"** in feminist research was coined by **Nancy Kleiber and Linda Light** (MCQ 2023).
16. "**Subjugation of men**" is **not** an aim of Women's Studies — a trap option in MCQ 2023.
17. Removing **caste hierarchy** is **not** an objective of a **gender-sensitisation** programme (MCQ 2023).
18. **Intersectionality** — the idea that gender overlaps with class, race and religion — was coined by **Kimberlé Crenshaw** in ==1989==.
19. Pakistan acceded to **CEDAW** in ==1996== and has promised **SDG 5** (gender equality) by ==2030== — both need gender expertise.
20. Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the **Global Gender Gap Report 2026** (after ==148th of 148== in 2025).

### B · Women's Studies and Gender Studies

21. One of the first **Women's Studies** courses was taught at **Cornell University** in ==1969==.
22. The first full Women's Studies **programme** began at **San Diego State College** in ==1970==.
23. The US **National Women's Studies Association** was founded in ==1977==.
24. Women's Studies was called "**the academic arm of the women's movement**".
25. Women's Studies made women the **subject**, the **knowers** and the **beneficiaries** of research.
26. India's report ***Towards Equality*** (==1974==) sparked Women's Studies in South Asia.
27. The key difference: Women's Studies studies **women**; Gender Studies studies **gender relations**, including **men** and **sexualities**.
28. **Gayle Rubin** (==1975==) described the **"sex/gender system"** in her essay "The Traffic in Women".
29. **Raewyn Connell**'s *Gender and Power* (==1987==) helped bring **men's studies** into the field.
30. **bell hooks** (==1981==) and **Chandra Talpade Mohanty** (==1984==) criticised Women's Studies for treating white, Western women as "all women".
31. **Tania Modleski**'s *Feminism Without Women* (==1991==) warned that "gender" could make **women invisible again**.
32. Many departments use the joint name "**Women's and Gender Studies**" to keep both the focus on women and the study of relations.

### C · The multidisciplinary nature

33. **Multidisciplinary** means disciplines working **side by side**; **interdisciplinary** means **mixing** their methods into one approach.
34. **Transdisciplinary** work goes **beyond the university**, working with activists and communities.
35. **Ann Oakley** (*The Sociology of Housework*, ==1974==) showed that housework is **real, unpaid work**.
36. **Margaret Mead** (==1935==) studied the **Arapesh, Mundugumor and Tchambuli** of New Guinea.
37. **Sherry Ortner** (==1974==) asked "**Is Female to Male as Nature Is to Culture?**"
38. **Michelle Rosaldo** (==1974==) linked women's lower status to their confinement to the **domestic sphere**.
39. **Gerda Lerner** wrote ***The Creation of Patriarchy*** (==1986==), showing that male rule had a historical beginning.
40. **Ester Boserup** (*Woman's Role in Economic Development*, ==1970==) showed that development ignored **women farmers**.
41. **Carole Pateman** wrote ***The Sexual Contract*** (==1988==) — the liberal "social contract" hid a contract subordinating women.
42. **Catharine MacKinnon** (==1979==) argued that **sexual harassment is sex discrimination**.
43. **Laura Mulvey** (==1975==) coined the **"male gaze"** in film.
44. **Elaine Showalter** coined **gynocriticism** — the study of women writers (MCQ 2024).

### D · Autonomy versus integration

45. The **autonomy/integration debate** was framed by **Gloria Bowles and Renate Duelli Klein** in ***Theories of Women's Studies*** (==1983==).
46. **Autonomy** means a **separate department** with its own staff, courses and degrees.
47. **Integration** (**mainstreaming**) means **adding gender to every subject**.
48. The main risk of autonomy is **ghettoisation** — the field becomes a small, ignored "women's corner".
49. The main risk of integration is **dilution** — gender reduced to **one token lecture**.
50. Bowles and Klein argued that integration **without an autonomous base** would water down feminist study.
51. The usual answer to the debate is the **dual strategy**: a separate base that **drives integration** into other subjects.
52. The **Beijing Platform for Action** (==1995==) used a "**twin-track**" approach — mainstreaming plus special measures — the policy version of the dual strategy.
53. **Sandra Harding** (*The Science Question in Feminism*, ==1986==) asked whether "objective" science reflects **male viewpoints** — a product of the integration debate.
54. In Pakistan, **autonomy came first** (the 1989 centres) while **integration** into law, medicine and economics remains **weak**.

### E · Gender Studies in Pakistan

55. Women's Studies centres were set up in **five public universities** in ==1989== by the **Ministry of Women's Development**.
56. The five universities: **Quaid-i-Azam** (Islamabad), **Karachi**, **Punjab** (Lahore), **Peshawar** and **Balochistan** (Quetta).
57. A **Centre of Excellence in Gender Studies** is housed at **Quaid-i-Azam University** (MCQ 2016).
58. The ***Pakistan Journal of Gender Studies*** was the journal named in MCQ 2018.
59. **Shirkat Gah** (==1975==) and the **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==) did research on women **before** the university centres.
60. **Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed** wrote ***Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?*** (==1987==).
61. The **National Commission on the Status of Women** was set up in ==2000==.
62. The **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** was launched in ==2002== (MCQ 2018).
63. Gender Studies became a **CSS optional subject** from ==CE-2016== (Group V, code 31).
64. **Rubina Saigol** and **Farzana Bari** wrote the reviews of Women's Studies in Pakistan on the FPSC reading list.
65. The field's problems are **financial** (donor funding), **intellectual** (borrowed theory) and **political** ("Western agenda" charge).
66. The best answer to the "**Western agenda**" charge is **local research, local theory and local languages**.

### F · Constructionism and its theories

67. **Essentialism** holds that women and men have a **fixed, natural essence**; **constructionism** holds that gender is **made by society**.
68. **Aristotle** described the female as a kind of **incomplete male** — classic essentialism.
69. **Mary Wollstonecraft** (==1792==) argued women's weakness came from **lack of education**.
70. **J. S. Mill** (*The Subjection of Women*, ==1869==) called women's "nature" "**an eminently artificial thing**".
71. "**One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman**" was written by **Simone de Beauvoir** in ***The Second Sex*** (==1949==) (MCQs 2016, 2019, 2024, 2025).
72. Beauvoir argued that society treats woman as "**the Other**".
73. **John Money** first used the term **"gender role"** in ==1955==.
74. **Robert Stoller** (*Sex and Gender*, ==1968==) separated biological sex from **gender identity**.
75. **Ann Oakley** (*Sex, Gender and Society*, ==1972==) brought the **sex/gender distinction** into sociology.
76. **Berger and Luckmann** wrote ***The Social Construction of Reality*** (==1966==): externalisation, objectivation, internalisation.
77. **Harold Garfinkel** (==1967==) studied "**Agnes**", showing gender is an ongoing **accomplishment**.
78. **Foucault**'s *History of Sexuality* (==1976==) argued that **power produces discourse on sexuality**, rejecting the "repressive hypothesis" (MCQ 2026).
79. "**Doing Gender**" (==1987==) was proposed by **West and Zimmerman** (MCQ 2026).
80. **Judith Butler** wrote ***Gender Trouble*** (==1990==) (MCQ 2019) and argued gender is **performative** (MCQ 2024).
81. Performativity means gender is **constituted through repeated acts**, not an inner reality — "None of these" was the key in MCQ 2026.
82. **Judith Lorber** (*Paradoxes of Gender*, ==1994==) described gender as a **social institution**.
83. **Kohlberg**'s three stages: **gender identity** (2–3), **stability** (4–5), **constancy** (6–7).
84. **Sandra Bem** proposed **gender schema theory** (==1981==); **Bandura** and **Mischel** stand for **social learning theory**.

### G · The meanings of "gender" and gendered language

85. **Deconstruction** — exposing hidden rankings in **pairs of opposites** — comes from **Jacques Derrida** (==1967==).
86. The word "gender" comes from the Latin ***genus*** ("kind").
87. "Gender" has at least **eight meanings**: grammar, sex, roles, identity, performance, institution, **power**, policy label.
88. **Barbara Risman** (==2004==) called gender a **social structure** working at individual, interaction and institution levels.
89. **Robin Lakoff** wrote ***Language and Woman's Place*** (==1975==) — women taught hedges and tag questions.
90. **Dale Spender** wrote ***Man Made Language*** (==1980==) — men control the meaning of words.
91. **Deborah Tannen** (==1990==) contrasted women's **"rapport talk"** with men's **"report talk"**.
92. **Muriel Schulz** (==1975==) described the **semantic derogation** of women's words (master/mistress; bachelor/spinster).
93. "**Mankind**" and "**chairman**" are examples of the **generic masculine**.
94. "**Lady doctor**" is a **marked** female term — it treats the woman as the exception.
95. Pakistan's **2016** law speaks of crimes "in the name or **pretext** of honour", calling honour an **excuse**, not a justification.

### H · Queer theory and "sex"

96. The term **"queer theory"** was coined by **Teresa de Lauretis** (==1990–91==).
97. Queer theory is **not** "a new term for gays and lesbians" — the incorrect statement in MCQ 2023.
98. **Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick** wrote ***Epistemology of the Closet*** (==1990==).
99. **Heteronormativity** is the assumption of **two opposite genders** and that only heterosexuality is normal.
100. **Thomas Laqueur** (*Making Sex*, ==1990==) described Europe's shift from a **one-sex** to a **two-sex** model.
101. **Anne Fausto-Sterling** wrote "**The Five Sexes**" (==1993==) and *Sexing the Body* (==2000==).
102. Estimates of **intersex** births range from about **0.018%** (Sax) to **1.7%** (Fausto-Sterling), depending on the definition.
103. Butler suggested that "sex" may be "**always already gender**".
104. Classical Islamic law recognised the intersex person as ***khuntha***.

### I · Masculinities and femininities

105. **Hegemonic masculinity** was coined by **R. W. (Raewyn) Connell** (MCQs 2018, 2023, 2026).
106. Connell's four masculinities: **hegemonic, complicit, marginalised, subordinated**.
107. The **patriarchal dividend** is the advantage **all men** gain from women's subordination (Connell).
108. **Emphasised femininity** is femininity built around **accommodating men**.
109. An **androgynous** person has **both masculine and feminine** traits (MCQ 2023) — **positive** traits of both (MCQ 2024).
110. **Sandra Bem** measured androgyny with the **Bem Sex-Role Inventory** (==1974==).
111. **Engels** (==1884==) called women's subordination "the **world historical defeat of the female sex**".
112. Boserup's **plough** thesis: plough farming produced sharper gender roles (supported by **Alesina, Giuliano and Nunn**, ==2013==).

### J · Nature versus culture

113. The **evolutionary neuroandrogenic theory** (Lee Ellis) holds that gender differences in crime are **universal** (MCQ 2018).
114. In "**Baby X**" studies (Condry and Condry, ==1976==), adults saw a crying baby as "angry" if told it was a boy, "afraid" if a girl.
115. **Cordelia Fine** wrote ***Delusions of Gender*** (==2010==); **Daphna Joel** (==2015==) described the brain as a **"mosaic"**.
116. The **David Reimer** case (injured ==1966==) shows the limits of the "pure nurture" view.
117. Most scholars today take an **interactionist** view: biology gives tendencies, culture shapes them.

### K · The transgender debate

118. The **Supreme Court** directed recognition of a **third gender** on CNICs in **December** ==2009== (MCQ 2018).
119. **Pakistan** is a country that **legally recognises a third gender** (MCQ 2018).
120. The British **Criminal Tribes Act** (==1871==) labelled "eunuchs" a criminal class.
121. The **2017 Census** counted ==10,418== transgender persons.
122. The **Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act** was passed in ==2018==; **section 3** allowed **self-perceived** gender identity.
123. The **Federal Shariat Court** held sections **2(f), 3 and 7** of the Act against Islamic injunctions on ==19 May 2023==; appeals remained **pending** in 2026.
